<section xml:id="sec-early-stopping-control">
  <title>Continue, Break, &amp; Return</title>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-continue-break-return">
		<title>Syntax and Usage</title>
		<p>
			MATLAB provides commands that modify the normal flow of code. In particular, you can skip the current loop iteration or even exit a loop entirely. It is also possible to end a function early if your code already knows the output.
		</p>

		<p>
			These flow-control commands are known as <term>continue</term>, <term>break</term>, and <term>return</term>. They are used in specific contexts to alter the execution of loops and functions. The table below summarizes their effects:
		</p>

		<table xml:id="tab-loop-flow-control">
			<title>Flow control commands for loops</title>
			<tabular>
				<row header="yes">
					<cell>Command</cell>
					<cell>Effect</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>continue</pf></cell>
					<cell>Skip the rest of the current iteration and move to the next iteration.</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>break</pf></cell>
					<cell>Exit the loop immediately and continue after <pf>end</pf>.</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>return</pf></cell>
					<cell>Exit the current script or function immediately.</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table>

		<p>
			Important notes:
			<ul marker="square">
				<li>These commands only execute if MATLAB reaches the line where they appear.</li>
				<li><pf>continue</pf> and <pf>break</pf> may only be used inside a <xref ref="sec-for-loop" text="custom"><pf>for</pf>-loop</xref> or <xref ref="sec-while-loop" text="custom"><pf>while</pf>-loop</xref>; using them elsewhere causes an error.</li>
				<li><pf>return</pf> can be used anywhere inside a script or function to exit early.</li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<example>
			<title>Searching with break</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					A loop can search for a target value and stop early when found. The <pf>break</pf> command exits the loop immediately.
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
				target = 10;

				found = false;
				location = -1;

				for k = 1:50
					value = randi([-20 20]);   % generate one random integer

					if value == target
						found = true;
						location = k;
						break
					end
				end

				if found
					fprintf('The number %i was found on iteration %i\n', target, location)
				else
					fprintf('The number %i was not found in 50 trials\n', target)
				end
				</program>

				<p>
					This pattern—test a condition, then exit early with <pf>break</pf>—is useful whenever you want to stop searching once you find what you need.
				</p>
			</statement>

		</example>

		<example xml:id="example-continue-skip-multiples">
			<title>Skipping Multiples with continue</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					Sometimes you want to skip certain iterations in a loop without exiting early. The <pf>continue</pf> command jumps to the next iteration immediately, skipping any remaining code in the current iteration.
				</p>

				<p>
					For example, suppose you want to print all numbers from 1 to 10, but skip multiples of 3:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					for num = 1:10
						if mod(num, 3) == 0
							continue
						end
						fprintf('%i ', num)
					end
					fprintf('\n')
				</program>

				<p>
					Output: <c>1 2 4 5 7 8 10</c>
				</p>

				<p>
					When <pf>num</pf> is 3, 6, or 9, the <pf>continue</pf> command skips the <pf>fprintf</pf> statement and moves directly to the next value. This is cleaner than nesting the print statement inside an <pf>else</pf> block.
				</p>
			</statement>

		</example>

		<example xml:id="example-return-early-exit">
			<title>Early Exit with return</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					When writing a function that searches for something, you can use <pf>return</pf> to exit the function as soon as the answer is known. Unlike <pf>break</pf>, which only exits the loop, <pf>return</pf> exits the entire function.
				</p>

				<p>
					For example, this function checks whether a positive integer is even by testing if it is divisible by 2:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					function result = isEven(n)
						if mod(n, 2) == 0
							result = 1;
							return          % exit immediately; no need to continue
						end
						result = 0;
					end
				</program>

				<p>
					If <pf>n</pf> is even, MATLAB sets <pf>result = 1</pf> and exits immediately via <pf>return</pf>, skipping the line <pf>result = 0</pf>. If <pf>n</pf> is odd, MATLAB never enters the <pf>if</pf> block, so it reaches <pf>result = 0</pf> normally.
				</p>

				<p>
					The <pf>return</pf> command is especially useful inside loops. Here is a function that finds the smallest divisor of <pf>n</pf> greater than 1:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					function d = smallestDivisor(n)
						for d = 2:n
							if mod(n, d) == 0
								return      % d is already set; exit the function
							end
						end
					end
				</program>

				<p>
					The moment a divisor is found, <pf>return</pf> exits the function immediately. There is no need to continue checking the remaining values.
				</p>
			</statement>

		</example>

		<exercise label="continue-break-return-reading-ckpt-4">

			<task label="continue-break-return-reading-ckpt-4a">
				<title>What does continue do?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What does the <pf>continue</pf> command do in a loop?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								Skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to the next iteration.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>continue</pf> command immediately jumps to the next iteration, skipping any remaining code in the current iteration.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Exits the loop completely.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								This describes <pf>break</pf>, not <pf>continue</pf>. The <pf>continue</pf> command stays in the loop but skips to the next iteration.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Restarts the loop from the beginning.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>continue</pf> command moves to the next iteration, not back to the first one.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Exits the current function or script.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								This describes <pf>return</pf>, not <pf>continue</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="continue-break-return-reading-ckpt-4b">
				<title>Break exits the loop</title>
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						The <pf>break</pf> command exits the loop immediately and continues execution after the <pf>end</pf> statement.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Unlike <pf>continue</pf> which skips to the next iteration, <pf>break</pf> completely exits the loop.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="continue-break-return-reading-ckpt-4c">
				<title>Where can continue and break be used?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Where can the <pf>continue</pf> and <pf>break</pf> commands be used?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								Only inside <pf>for</pf>- or <pf>while</pf>-loops.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								These commands only make sense inside loops. Using them elsewhere causes an error.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Anywhere in a script or function.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								This describes <pf>return</pf>. The <pf>continue</pf> and <pf>break</pf> commands require a loop context.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Only inside <pf>if</pf>-statements.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								While often used with <pf>if</pf>-statements, they must be inside a loop, not just an <pf>if</pf>-statement.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Only at the end of a loop.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								These commands can appear anywhere inside the loop body.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="continue-break-return-reading-ckpt-4d">
				<title>When does return exit?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What does the <pf>return</pf> command do?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Exits only the current loop.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								This describes <pf>break</pf>. The <pf>return</pf> command exits the entire script or function.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								Exits the current script or function immediately.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>return</pf> command stops execution of the entire script or function, not just a loop.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Returns to the start of the loop.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>return</pf> command doesn't return to any previous location; it exits completely.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Skips to the next iteration.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								This describes <pf>continue</pf>, not <pf>return</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

		</exercise>
	</subsection>

	<reading-questions xml:id="early-stopping-control-reading-quiz">
		<title>📖 Reading Quiz</title>

		<exercise label="concepts-continue-break-return">

			<task label="cqs-continue-break-return-5">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which situation is the best reason to use <pf>break</pf> inside a <pf>for</pf>-loop?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>You want to stop early when you have found what you were looking for.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p><pf>break</pf> is for ending the loop early once a goal is reached.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>You want to skip just one iteration but keep looping.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Skipping a single iteration is what <pf>continue</pf> does.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>You want the loop variable to update automatically each iteration.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>The loop variable updates automatically without needing <pf>break</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>You want to print output on a new line.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>New lines depend on the format string (for example, including <pf>\n</pf>), not on <pf>break</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-continue-break-return-6">
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the difference between <pf>break</pf> and <pf>return</pf> when used inside a loop that is inside a function?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>break</pf> exits the loop but stays in the function; <pf>return</pf> exits the entire function.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>After <pf>break</pf>, MATLAB continues with code after the loop's <pf>end</pf>. After <pf>return</pf>, MATLAB goes back to whoever called the function.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>They do the same thing inside a function.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>They are different. <pf>break</pf> exits only the loop, while <pf>return</pf> exits the entire function.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>return</pf> exits the loop; <pf>break</pf> exits the function.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>This is backwards. <pf>break</pf> exits the loop, and <pf>return</pf> exits the function.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>break</pf> can only be used in <pf>for</pf>-loops, while <pf>return</pf> can only be used in <pf>while</pf>-loops.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Both <pf>break</pf> and <pf>return</pf> can be used in either type of loop.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-continue-break-return-7">
				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						The <pf>continue</pf> command can be used inside an <pf>if</pf>-statement that is not inside a loop.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						The <pf>continue</pf> command can only be used inside a <pf>for</pf>- or <pf>while</pf>-loop. Using it inside an <pf>if</pf>-statement that is not within a loop causes an error.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="drill-continue-break-return-trace-output">
			<title>Trace the Output</title>

			<task label="early-stopping-control-trace-1">
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is printed?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						total = 0;
						for k = 1:10
							total = total + k;
							if total &gt;= 15
								break
							end
						end
						fprintf('k = %d, total = %d\n', k, total)
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>k = 5, total = 15</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>The running total is 1, 3, 6, 10, 15. On iteration <pf>k = 5</pf>, total reaches 15 and the <pf>break</pf> fires.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>k = 4, total = 10</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>After <pf>k = 4</pf>, total is 10 which is less than 15, so the loop continues.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>k = 6, total = 21</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>The <pf>break</pf> fires as soon as <pf>total &gt;= 15</pf>. That happens at <pf>k = 5</pf>, so iteration 6 never runs.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>k = 10, total = 55</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>The <pf>break</pf> exits the loop early. Without it, the loop would reach <pf>k = 10</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="early-stopping-control-control-trace-2">
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is printed?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						count = 0;
						for k = 1:12
							if mod(k,4) == 0
								continue
							end
							count = count + 1;
						end
						fprintf('count = %d\n', count)
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>count = 9</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Multiples of 4 in 1–12 are 4, 8, and 12 (three values). Those iterations skip the increment, so count = 12 − 3 = 9.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>count = 3</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>This would be the number of values <em>skipped</em>, not the number counted. The <pf>continue</pf> skips the increment, so 12 − 3 = 9 values are counted.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>count = 12</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>The <pf>continue</pf> skips the <pf>count = count + 1</pf> line for multiples of 4, so the count is less than 12.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>count = 8</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>There are 3 multiples of 4 in 1–12 (namely 4, 8, 12), so count = 12 − 3 = 9.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="early-stopping-control-control-trace-3">
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is printed?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						total = 0;
						for n = 1:8
							if mod(n, 2) == 0
								continue
							end
							total = total + n;
						end
						fprintf('total = %d\n', total)
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>total = 16</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Even values (2, 4, 6, 8) are skipped. The odd values 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 = 16 are summed.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>total = 20</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>The even values are skipped by <pf>continue</pf>. Only odd values are added: 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 = 16.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>total = 36</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>This would be the sum of all values 1–8 = 36. But the even values are skipped.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>total = 1</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>The loop does not stop at the first odd number. It continues through all values 1–8, only adding the odd ones.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="drill-continue-break-return-code-build" language="octave" numbered="left" indentation="hide">
			<title>Build the Code</title>

			<task label="drill-continue-break-return-code-build-a" language="octave" numbered="left" indentation="hide">
				<title>Find first multiple of 7</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Put the blocks in order to print the first multiple of 7 between 1 and 50.
					</p>
					<p>
						<em>Use correct indentation to show which lines are inside the loop and if statement.</em>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<blocks>
					<block><cline>first = NaN;			</cline></block>
					<block><cline>for k = 1:50							</cline></block>
					<block><cline>	if mod(k, 7) == 0				</cline></block>
					<block><cline>		first = k;		</cline></block>
					<block><cline>		break									</cline></block>
					<block><cline>	end											</cline></block>
					<block><cline>end												</cline>
								<cline>fprintf('%i\n', first)	</cline></block>
				</blocks>
			</task>

			<task label="drill-continue-break-return-code-build-b" language="octave" numbered="left" indentation="hide">
				<title>Sum non-multiples of 5</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Put the blocks in order to compute the sum of all integers from 1 to 30 that are <em>not</em> multiples of 5. Use <pf>continue</pf> to skip the multiples.
					</p>
					<p>
						<em>Use correct indentation to show which lines are inside the loop and if statement.</em>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<blocks>
					<block><cline>total = 0;									</cline></block>
					<block><cline>for k = 1:30								</cline></block>
					<block><cline>	if mod(k, 5) == 0					</cline></block>
					<block><cline>		continue							</cline></block>
					<block><cline>	end											</cline></block>
					<block><cline>	total = total + k;				</cline></block>
					<block><cline>end												</cline>
								<cline>fprintf('total = %i\n', total)</cline></block>
				</blocks>
			</task>

		</exercise>

	</reading-questions>

	<reading-questions xml:id="early-stopping-control-control-coding-practice">
		<title>🧑🏻‍💻 Coding Practice</title>

		<exercise label="continue-drills">
			<title>Using <pf>continue</pf> in a Loop</title>

			<task label="activity-continue-control-flow-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a <pf>for</pf>-loop from 1 to 20 that adds the loop index to a running total. Use an <pf>if</pf>-statement with <pf>mod</pf> and <pf>continue</pf> so that values divisible by 3 are skipped.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							total = 0;
							for k = 1:20
								%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%


								%───────────────────────────%
								total = total + k;
							end
							fprintf('total = %i\n', total);
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						When done, evaluate and confirm it prints <pf>total = 147</pf>. (The sum of integers 1–20 is 210, and the multiples of 3 from 1–20 sum to 63, so 210 − 63 = 147.)
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						total = 0;
						for k = 1:20
							if mod(k, 3) == 0
								continue
							end
							total = total + k;
						end
						fprintf('total = %i\n', total);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-continue-control-flow-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Modify your script to also count how many values were skipped. Print both the final total and the number of skipped values.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							total = 0;
							skipped = 0;
							for k = 1:20
								%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%


								%───────────────────────────%
								total = total + k;
							end
							fprintf('total = %i, skipped = %i\n', total, skipped);
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						When done, evaluate and confirm it prints <pf>total = 147, skipped = 6</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						total = 0;
						skipped = 0;
						for k = 1:20
							if mod(k, 3) == 0
								skipped = skipped + 1;
								continue
							end
							total = total + k;
						end
						fprintf('total = %i, skipped = %i\n', total, skipped);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="break-drills">
			<title>Using <pf>break</pf> in a Loop</title>

			<task label="activity-break-control-flow-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a <pf>for</pf>-loop that generates random integers with <pf>randi([1, 10])</pf> for up to 20 trials. Use an <pf>if</pf>-statement with <pf>break</pf> to stop as soon as the value is 7. Print the trial number where 7 was found, or report that it was not found.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							rng(42); % Set seed for reproducibility
							found = false;
							%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%


							%───────────────────────────%
							if found
								fprintf('Found 7 on trial %i\n', trial);
							else
								fprintf('7 was not found in 20 trials\n');
							end
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						rng(42);
						found = false;
						for trial = 1:20
							value = randi([1, 10]);
							if value == 7
								found = true;
								break
							end
						end
						if found
							fprintf('Found 7 on trial %i\n', trial);
						else
							fprintf('7 was not found in 20 trials\n');
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-break-control-flow-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Rewrite the previous task using a <pf>while</pf>-loop instead of a <pf>for</pf>-loop. Use a counter variable <pf>trial</pf> and stop when either the value is 7 or the counter reaches 15. Report whether the target was found and how many trials were used.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							rng(42); % Set seed for reproducibility
							found = false;
							trial = 0;
							%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%


							%───────────────────────────%
							if found
								fprintf('Found 7 on trial %i\n', trial);
							else
								fprintf('7 not found after %i trials\n', trial);
							end
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						rng(42);
						found = false;
						trial = 0;
						while trial &lt; 15
							trial = trial + 1;
							value = randi([1, 10]);
							if value == 7
								found = true;
								break
							end
						end
						if found
							fprintf('Found 7 on trial %i\n', trial);
						else
							fprintf('7 not found after %i trials\n', trial);
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="return-drills">
			<title>Using <pf>return</pf> in a Function</title>

			<task label="return-drills-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function named <pf>firstMultipleOf</pf> that takes inputs <pf>d</pf> and <pf>limit</pf> and uses a <pf>for</pf>-loop to find the first multiple of <pf>d</pf> between 1 and <pf>limit</pf>. Use <pf>return</pf> inside the loop to exit the function immediately when the first multiple is found.
					</p>
					<p>
						If no multiple exists in the range, return <pf>-1</pf>.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
							function result = firstMultipleOf(d, limit)
								result = -1;
								% your loop here

							end
							%───────────────────────────%
							fprintf('%i\n', firstMultipleOf(6, 20));   % expected: 6
							fprintf('%i\n', firstMultipleOf(7, 5));    % expected: -1
							fprintf('%i\n', firstMultipleOf(13, 100)); % expected: 13
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function result = firstMultipleOf(d, limit)
							result = -1;
							for k = 1:limit
								if mod(k, d) == 0
									result = k;
									return
								end
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="return-drills-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function named <pf>hasRepeatedDigit</pf> that takes a positive integer <pf>n</pf> and returns <pf>1</pf> if any digit appears more than once, and <pf>0</pf> otherwise. Use <pf>return</pf> to exit early as soon as a repeated digit is found.
					</p>
					<p>
						One approach: use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to extract digits. For each digit, use a second loop or a counter to check whether you have already seen it. If so, <pf>return</pf> immediately.
					</p>

					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%


							%───────────────────────────%
							fprintf('%i\n', hasRepeatedDigit(1234));   % expected: 0
							fprintf('%i\n', hasRepeatedDigit(1214));   % expected: 1
							fprintf('%i\n', hasRepeatedDigit(11));     % expected: 1
							fprintf('%i\n', hasRepeatedDigit(9));      % expected: 0
						</input>
					</sage>

					<hint>
						<p>
							You can count occurrences of a specific digit <pf>d</pf> in <pf>n</pf> by looping through all digits of <pf>n</pf> and counting how many times <pf>mod(temp, 10) == d</pf>.
						</p>
					</hint>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						One approach: extract each digit, then for each one, count how many times it appears in the full number.
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						function result = hasRepeatedDigit(n)
							temp = n;
							while temp &gt; 0
								d = mod(temp, 10);
								% Count how many times d appears in n
								count = 0;
								check = n;
								while check &gt; 0
									if mod(check, 10) == d
										count = count + 1;
									end
									check = floor(check / 10);
								end
								if count &gt; 1
									result = 1;
									return
								end
								temp = floor(temp / 10);
							end
							result = 0;
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>
	</reading-questions>

</section>